### tool_provider.http — same format as your composio.http reference
### (/home/gr/projects/old_repo/ai-agent-backend/.idea/composio.http), works in JetBrains' HTTP Client and
### VS Code's REST Client. Comparison below is against Composio's REAL v3 API — fetched directly from
### https://backend.composio.dev/api/v3/openapi.json (74 real endpoints, not inferred from docs prose),
### not just the core/lib/composio wrapper. Full generated API docs for THIS server: GET {{base_url}}/docs
### (Swagger UI, schemas generated live from the actual zod definitions in src/components/*/actions/*.ts —
### can't drift out of sync with the code, see src/openapi.ts's header comment).
###
### This file only covers general/account endpoints + ONE example action per app, as a quick smoke test.
### The FULL per-app flow — connect, every action, every trigger (subscribe/unsubscribe) — lives in its own
### .http file, one per component, mirroring src/components/<app>/ 1:1:
###   test_components/gmail/gmail.http
###   test_components/slack/slack.http
### (SerpApi has only one action total, so it isn't split out — see the one example below.)
###
### For Gmail triggers to actually fire, `bun run worker` (worker.ts) must be running alongside `bun run
### dev` (the API server, this base_url) — Gmail polling happens in a separate process, see PHASES.md
### Phase 3. Slack's new_message trigger needs no worker — it's delivered by the API server itself via
### POST {{base_url}}/webhooks/slack/events, registered as the Request URL in Slack's own app config.

@base_url = http://localhost:3005

### Paste your real values from .env here (ACCESS_TOKEN / ADMIN_ACCESS_TOKEN) — every gated route now
### requires a real 'Authorization: Bearer <token>' header, no ?token= query-param fallback anymore.
@access_token = 3772288b374e7045c59364c8dcad4be12dc1338cf0a80d6dee42ac2adaa392d1
@admin_access_token = b9fa8a575619f0c6d62c202b8946a57746e9b972e465a169349e6f8684734add

# ============================================================================
# COMPARISON — what we have vs. Composio's real API surface (74 endpoints total)
# ============================================================================
#
# ---- We implement (real, tested against live Google/Slack/SerpApi + real local Redis) ---------------
# POST   /users                              (no Composio equivalent — see "EXTRA" below)
# POST   /connections                        ~ POST /connected_accounts/link — NEVER carries secrets or a
#                                              field schema, for ANY auth type. Response is always just
#                                              {connection_id, status, connect_url?, app} — oauth2 AND
#                                              api_key both return `connect_url` (identical shape); none ->
#                                              active immediately, no connect_url.
# GET/POST /connect/{token}                  ~ oauth2: /connected_accounts/link's redirect_url, but proxied
#                                              through our own domain first. api_key: no Composio MCP-side
#                                              equivalent — this is OUR OWN server-rendered field-collection
#                                              page (plain HTML form, no JS). `token` is a random,
#                                              single-use, Redis-backed value (src/lib/redis.ts) — the raw
#                                              connection_id never appears in a URL handed to an end user.
#                                              POST here (the form's own submit) runs a REAL
#                                              testConnection() call (src/components/serpapi/app.ts) before
#                                              activating — a bad value re-renders the same form with the
#                                              real provider error, token stays valid to retry.
# GET    /connections/{id}                   ~ GET  /connected_accounts/{nanoid}
# GET    /connections                        ~ GET  /connected_accounts (list, filtered to `user_id`, +optional `app`)
# GET    /actions                            ~ GET  /tools (list/search)
# GET    /actions/{tool_slug}                ~ GET  /tools/{slug} (schema)
# POST   /actions/execute/{tool_slug}        ~ POST /tools/execute/{tool_slug} — SAME shape now: one flat
#                                              tool_slug (e.g. GMAIL_SEND_EMAIL), not two path segments.
# GET    /oauth/callback/{app}               (internal redirect target, not a public API either side)
# GET    /triggers                           ~ GET  /triggers_types — trigger TYPES (available to subscribe),
#                                              not instances. Bearer-token-gated same as everything else now
#                                              (src/lib/apiAuth.ts) — was unauthenticated at first pass.
# GET    /triggers/instances                 ~ no direct Composio equivalent — a user's subscribed
#                                              TriggerInstances, distinct from GET /triggers above
# POST   /triggers/{app}/{trigger}/subscribe ~ POST /trigger_instances/{slug}/upsert — but ALSO takes
#                                              webhook_url in the same call (see "Real gaps" below for the
#                                              webhook_subscriptions split Composio does instead)
# PATCH  /triggers/{id}                      (no Composio equivalent — extra_metadata-only update, the
#                                              client's own editable notes space on a TriggerInstance)
# DELETE /triggers/{id}                      ~ DELETE /trigger_instances/manage/{triggerId}
# GET    /triggers/instances/{id}/logs       ~ no direct Composio equivalent — per-instance delivery/poll
#                                              run history (status, webhook_url, error, resendable, and by
#                                              default the full delivered `payload` inlined — no second
#                                              GET /triggers/logs/{id} call needed per row; ?with_payload=false
#                                              to skim without it, ?limit=N to page, default 20)
# GET    /triggers/logs                      ~ no direct Composio equivalent — same as the line above but
#                                              across EVERY trigger instance this user has, not just one;
#                                              each row carries its own trigger_instance_id/app/trigger_key
# GET    /triggers/logs/{log_id}             ~ GET  /v1/events/{id} (Stripe) — one event's full body,
#                                              including its payload; `log_id` IS the event id for a
#                                              delivery row (src/core/scheduler.ts's deliverEvent/
#                                              resendDelivery generate one `evt_...` id, used as both)
# POST   /triggers/logs/{log_id}/resend      ~ Stripe CLI's `events resend` — re-POSTs an already-captured
#                                              delivery's exact payload to the instance's CURRENT webhook_url
#
# ---- Composio has, we DON'T (real gaps) ------------------------------------
# Auth Configs (full CRUD)        POST/GET/PATCH/DELETE /auth_configs, PATCH .../{status}
#   -> we hardcode the "auth config" in code (src/components/<app>/app.ts) — no API to create/inspect/
#      disable one. Fine at 3 apps; would matter for Phase 5 (bring-your-own OAuth app per auth-patterns.md #3).
# Delete/disable a Connection     DELETE /connected_accounts/{id}, PATCH .../status
#   -> CLOSED (Phase 7): DELETE /connections/{id}?user_id= (real revoke: status -> "revoked" + secrets
#      cleared, not cosmetic) + MCP's disconnect_connection tool. No separate enable/disable status toggle —
#      disconnect + reconnect (manage_connection) covers that today, same as trigger pause/resume below.
# Refresh a Connection            POST /connected_accounts/{nanoid}/refresh
#   -> we don't expose a manual-refresh endpoint, but it's not a gap: src/lib/oauth.ts's refreshToken() is
#      real and wired into every action/poll call via src/core/tokenRefresh.ts's ensureFreshConnection()
#      (refresh-ahead-of-expiry, Phase 4), so a caller never needs to ask for one manually.
# Toolkit/Tool introspection      GET /toolkits, /toolkits/{slug}, /toolkits/categories, /tools, /tools/{slug}, /tools/enum
#   -> partially closed: GET /actions (~ /tools, list/search by ?q=) and GET /actions/{tool_slug}
#      (~ /tools/{slug}, real schema) now exist — see below. Still missing: /toolkits (app-level listing —
#      we only expose the flattened tool level) and /toolkits/categories (no category concept here).
# Natural-language tool input     POST /tools/execute/{tool_slug}/input
#   -> not applicable at our scale (this is Composio's "let an LLM turn free text into typed arguments"
#      helper, useful across 1000s of tools; less valuable across 10).
# Trigger enable/disable (pause without deleting)   PATCH /trigger_instances/manage/{id}
#   -> we have create (subscribe) + delete (unsubscribe), real and working. No pause/resume — delete and
#      re-subscribe is the only option today.
# Tool Router (session-based dynamic tool use)   POST /tool_router/session + 10 sub-routes
#   -> philosophically different, not just missing: Composio's Tool Router lets an agent search/discover
#      tools at runtime across thousands of toolkits. We don't have (and at ~25 actions, don't need) a
#      discovery layer — every action is already known/declared, called directly by app+key.
# Webhook Endpoints / Subscriptions (outbound delivery config)   full CRUD, both namespaces
#   -> matches our own gap: research/triggers-patterns.md's "Phase 6, not decided yet" fan-in-vs-per-trigger
#      question. No outbound webhook delivery of any kind exists here yet.
# Proxy execute                   POST /tools/execute/proxy
#   -> Composio can proxy an arbitrary authenticated HTTP call through a connected account without a
#      pre-defined "tool" wrapping it. We have no equivalent — every call must go through a declared Action.
#
# ---- We have, Composio DOESN'T (extras) ------------------------------------
# POST /users                     Composio has no user resource at all — you just pass any user_id string
#                                  you choose directly into connected_accounts/link. We model User as a
#                                  real record (src/types.ts) with opaque metadata attached.
# GET/POST /connect/{token}       Composio's /connected_accounts/link returns the provider's redirect_url
#                                  DIRECTLY to the caller (oauth2), or expects credentials already collected
#                                  at auth-config-creation time (api_key). We interpose our own domain for
#                                  BOTH (per your spec — "get_connect_url will be our server link"): one
#                                  token-based URL that redirects (oauth2) or serves our own field-collection
#                                  form (api_key) — same shape either way, zero client-side UI work.
# GET /docs, GET /openapi.json    Composio's API doesn't serve its own docs — docs.composio.dev is a
#                                  separate, hand-maintained site. Ours is generated from the running
#                                  server's own code and served by the server itself.
# /admin/* (users/triggers/logs)  Composio has no equivalent read-only inspection API — that's dashboard-
#                                  UI-only on their side. Ours is API-first (still unimplemented, but the
#                                  shape exists) since we have no dashboard.
#
# ============================================================================

#### composio ->>> x-api-key: {{api_key}} on every request (a real platform API key)
#### but we have: `Authorization: Bearer <ACCESS_TOKEN>` (or ADMIN_ACCESS_TOKEN for /admin/*) on every
#### gated route (src/lib/apiAuth.ts) — set @access_token/@admin_access_token above from your .env.

### ----------------------------------------------------
### 🔧 API docs (Swagger UI) — generated live from this server's own zod schemas
### ----------------------------------------------------
GET {{base_url}}/docs

###
GET {{base_url}}/openapi.json
Authorization: Bearer {{access_token}}

### ----------------------------------------------------
### 🔧 Create a user
### ----------------------------------------------------
POST {{base_url}}/users
Authorization: Bearer {{access_token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "metadata": { "note": "created from tool_provider.http", "username": "Gaurav" }
}

### Copy the returned user_id into this variable for the requests below
@user_id = usr_bf9d74a9-c070-48ec-b3ad-bf18f0b24b67

### ----------------------------------------------------
### 🔧 Connect an app — oauth2 (Gmail) or api_key (SerpApi): IDENTICAL shape either way
#### composio ->>> two separate flows: oauth2 apps go through /auth_configs + /connected_accounts/link
#### (returns redirect_url); api_key apps go through /auth_configs with authScheme:"API_KEY" (credentials
#### passed at auth-config-creation time, not via a browser page).
#### but we have: ONE flow, same response shape for both. POST /connections never carries secrets or a
#### field schema — just {user_id, app, extra_metadata?} in, {connection_id, status, connect_url} out.
#### `connect_url` is a random, single-use, Redis-backed token (src/lib/redis.ts) — never the raw
#### connection_id. Open it in a browser: oauth2 apps redirect straight to Google/Slack's consent screen;
#### api_key apps get OUR OWN server-rendered field-collection form (src/api/connection_routes.ts's
#### fieldFormPage()) — no client-side UI work either way, the caller just gets a link to hand to the user.
### ----------------------------------------------------
POST {{base_url}}/connections
Authorization: Bearer {{access_token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "user_id": "{{user_id}}",
  "app": "gmail",
  "extra_metadata": { "note": "created from tool_provider.http" }
}

### Copy the returned connect_url and open it in a browser to finish OAuth. See test_components/gmail/gmail.http
### for the full Gmail action + trigger flow once connected.
